[{:db/id 17592186128938 :question/problem "The vector $\\mathbf{v}$ and the matrix $A$ are given below. \nIf $\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector of $A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=3,$ then what is the value of $\\dfrac{b}{c}?$\n\n![](images/65/65004c0c3788085f6f11fcdf9b107c43004dd4f7f21c6b1ecd78b81a89c76f46.svg)" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A - \\lambda I) \\textbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =3.$ So, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 & -6 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & -6 \\\\ 2 & -2 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-6{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}-4{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}-3{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -3 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{1}:=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -3 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd). Thus, $x_3$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain $x_2=x_3.$ From the first equation, we get $x_1=3x_3.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n![](images/a4/a4e3f32645ea033a5165c12ddb6a0563ae00f061e4e0f570ba739c531d28cd0b.svg)\n\nSetting, for instance, $x_3=1,$ we get the following eigenvector:\n\n![](images/b1/b1058a07d9ab58d8ec18ea08b8afb1e09e2dc97ae02f1289f730944fb075d370.svg)\n\nFinally, $\\dfrac{b}{c}=\\dfrac{1}{1}=1.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186129128 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-63a61ce2-0cf2-5d16-90c2-21d6b64fbbcd"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186128939 :question/problem "Find an eigenvector of\n![](images/ac/acc39fa601e9dd28da49849b9e3e098441f7d9dc2b9ad9c1b58af9ae7e014d98.svg)\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=-2.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A - \\lambda I) \\textbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =-2.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]-(-2)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}+4{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 0=0 \\\\ {x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}↔{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $3$rd). Thus, $x_2$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain $x_3=0.$ Substituting this in the first equation, we get $x_1=-x_2.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n![](images/31/31ec85b3cd3afae1b23c4558b7e2d8d758f9aff3ed6fb02760684e2f25ccc948.svg)\n\nSetting, for instance, $x_2=1,$ we get the eigenvector\n![](images/1c/1c22d6fa620b2de744da62b5e05088a571cb74172cf4e031a87dd90f74adb00b.svg)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186128940 :question/problem "![](images/e9/e93ca6621010628ace0d692899b04fb981045bd570d6bb68a502a03ab02064ce.svg)\n\nThe vectors $\\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2$ are eigenvectors that correspond to the eigenvalues $\\lambda_1=1$ and $\\lambda_2=-1$ of the matrix $A,$ given above. Find the third eigenvector $\\mathbf{v}_3=\\big[ a, \\, b, \\, c \\big]^T$ such that the set $\\{ \\mathbf{v}_1,\\mathbf{v}_2, \\mathbf{v}_3 \\}$ is linearly independent. What is the value of $\\dfrac{a}{c}?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is a diagonal matrix, we have that its eigenvalues are the entries in the main diagonal, that is, $\\lambda_1=1,$ $\\lambda_2=-1$ and $\\lambda_3=4.$ \n\nAlso, since we obtained three distinct eigenvalues, the set of eigenvectors $\\{ \\mathbf{v}_1,\\mathbf{v}_2, \\mathbf{v}_3 \\},$ where each vector corresponds to different eigenvalue, will be linearly independent.\n\nNow, recall that $\\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2$ correspond to the eigenvalues $\\lambda_1=1$ and $\\lambda_2=-1.$ Thus, the third eigenvector $\\mathbf{v}_3$ must correspond to $\\lambda_3=4.$ \n\nTo compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A - \\lambda I) \\textbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =4.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]-4[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-3{x}_{1}=0 \\\\ -5{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 0=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the augmented matrix $M$ is already in its row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this matrix, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd). Thus, $x_3$ is a free variable. From the first and second equations, we obtain $x_1=x_2=0.$  Therefore, the general solution is\n![](images/4b/4be28d6335835749df1f44a49e8cbe52eaceb3f2efbaccf6a085caa08d6d60ac.svg)\n\nSetting, for instance, $x_3=1,$ we get the following eigenvector:\n\n![](images/f7/f70927c82679024a0d9b58a2d30bb404968cac08bf2dca98f7e247742dad6f33.svg)\n\nFinally, $\\dfrac{a}{c}=\\dfrac{0}{1}=0.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186323866 :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =3.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 9 & -6 & -9 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 9 & -9 & -9 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 9{x}_{1}-9{x}_{2}-9{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}+2{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 9 & -9 & -9 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-9){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -9 & -27 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:=(-\\frac{1}{9}){R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd one). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-2{x}_{3}.$ From the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=-3{x}_{3}.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-2{x}_{3} \\\\ -3{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{3}=1,$ we get the following eigenvector:\n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{b}{c}=\\frac{-3}{1}=-3.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186323912 :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =1.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}21 & -13 & -9 \\\\ 20 & -12 & -9 \\\\ 18 & -12 & -7\\end{aligned}]-1[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}20 & -13 & -9 \\\\ 20 & -13 & -9 \\\\ 18 & -12 & -8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}20{x}_{1}-13{x}_{2}-9{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 20{x}_{1}-13{x}_{2}-9{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 18{x}_{1}-12{x}_{2}-8{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}20 & -13 & -9 & 0 \\\\ 20 & -13 & -9 & 0 \\\\ 18 & -12 & -8 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-\\frac{9}{10}){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}20 & -13 & -9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{3}{10} & \\frac{1}{10} & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}↔{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}20 & -13 & -9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{3}{10} & \\frac{1}{10} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{2}=\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{20}(13{x}_{2}+9{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{20}(13(\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{3})+9{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{3}=3,$ we get the following eigenvector:\n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{c}=\\frac{2}{3}.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186272148 :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =-3.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]-(-3)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 7\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}5{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ {x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 7{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 7 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-7){R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $3$rd). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{3}=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{-{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{-{x}_{2}+2(0)}{5} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{x}_{2}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{{x}_{2}}{5} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=5,$ we get the eigenvector\n$v=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-63a61ce2-0cf2-5d16-90c2-21d6b64fbbcd" :question/id #uuid "63a61ce2-0cf2-5d16-90c2-21d6b64fbbcd" :question/math-academy-id "q-89420" :question/problem "Find an eigenvector of $A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =4.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =4.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]-4[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -{x}_{2}-4{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -2{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-\\frac{2}{5}){R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $2$nd and the $3$rd). Thus, ${x}_{1}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{3}=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get ${x}_{2}=0.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{1}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{1}=1,$ we get the eigenvector\n$v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-3fe938d8-0558-5523-ac93-9554d3b69d6e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-3fe938d8-0558-5523-ac93-9554d3b69d6e" :answer-field/id #uuid "3fe938d8-0558-5523-ac93-9554d3b69d6e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-3fd03cb4-06c8-5ca2-9f08-a74b431e7f8f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-411fd766-6b79-5313-9896-cd722545c888" "answer-f0a25b95-c8b3-5a75-914b-884c50597eba" "answer-6387384c-e9f6-53af-81e8-9b9f29787d2f" "answer-3fd03cb4-06c8-5ca2-9f08-a74b431e7f8f" "answer-4a13fd92-b1e8-58ed-960f-c91130469a89"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-411fd766-6b79-5313-9896-cd722545c888" :answer/id #uuid "411fd766-6b79-5313-9896-cd722545c888" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f0a25b95-c8b3-5a75-914b-884c50597eba" :answer/id #uuid "f0a25b95-c8b3-5a75-914b-884c50597eba" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6387384c-e9f6-53af-81e8-9b9f29787d2f" :answer/id #uuid "6387384c-e9f6-53af-81e8-9b9f29787d2f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3fd03cb4-06c8-5ca2-9f08-a74b431e7f8f" :answer/id #uuid "3fd03cb4-06c8-5ca2-9f08-a74b431e7f8f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4a13fd92-b1e8-58ed-960f-c91130469a89" :answer/id #uuid "4a13fd92-b1e8-58ed-960f-c91130469a89" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186323867 :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are eigenvectors that correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=2$ of the matrix $A,$ given above. Find the third eigenvector ${v}_{3}=[a,\\,b,\\,c]^{T}$ such that the set $\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\}$ is linearly independent. What is the value of $\\frac{b}{c}?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is a diagonal matrix, we have that its eigenvalues are the entries in the main diagonal, that is, ${\\lambda }_{1}=3,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=2$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=-1.$ \n\nAlso, since we obtained three distinct eigenvalues, the set of eigenvectors $\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\},$ where each vector corresponds to different eigenvalue, will be linearly independent.\n\nNow, recall that ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=2.$ Thus, the third eigenvector ${v}_{3}$ must correspond to ${\\lambda }_{3}=-1.$ \n\nTo compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =-1.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]-(-1)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}4{x}_{1}=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 0=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the augmented matrix $M$ is already in row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd one). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the first and second equations, we obtain ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$  Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{3}=1,$ we get the following eigenvector:\n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{b}{c}=\\frac{0}{1}=0.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186272149 :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are eigenvectors that correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3$ of the matrix $A,$ given above. Find the third eigenvector ${v}_{3}=[a,\\,b,\\,c]^{T}$ such that the set $\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\}$ is linearly independent. What is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is an upper triangular matrix, we have that its eigenvalues are the entries in the main diagonal, that is, ${\\lambda }_{1}=2,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=1.$ \n\nAlso, since we obtained three distinct eigenvalues, the set of eigenvectors $\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\},$ where each vector corresponds to different eigenvalue, will be linearly independent.\n\nNow, recall that ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3.$ Thus, the third eigenvector ${v}_{3}$ must correspond to ${\\lambda }_{3}=1.$ \n\nTo compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =1.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]-1[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -4{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 0=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the augmented matrix $M$ is already in its row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{2}=\\frac{1}{4}{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =-{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}{x}_{3}+2{x}_{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{4}{x}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7}{4}{x}_{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{3}=4,$ we get the following eigenvector:\n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{7}{1}=7.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186130347 :tutorial/content "Consider the following $3\\times 3$ matrix: \n\n![](images/4f/4fd591d8028847aa7e1d11e840acdb192703d152f9c397e40fec98fd1e281c41.svg)\n\nIf we are told that one of the eigenvalues of this matrix is $\\lambda = 3,$ how do we calculate the corresponding eigenvector?\n\nTo compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation\n\n$$\n(A - \\lambda I) \\textbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =3.$ So, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 6 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -4\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 6 & -8 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -7\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, to find the eigenvectors, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}6{x}_{2}-8{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -3{x}_{2}+6{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{2}-7{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 6 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -7 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 6 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+\\frac{3}{2}{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 6 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this reduced matrix, there are two pivot columns (the $2$nd and $3$rd). Thus, $x_1$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we have $x_3 = 0.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain $x_2 = 0.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n![](images/70/705dd92c7330897f35671b66866791160b9d7f65e3280d5ccb9d992405c08942.svg)\n\nSetting $x_1=1,$ we get the following eigenvector:\n\n![](images/2a/2a5dd6c35fb81134f13e765e0a02be4ea2feedfd878a1923491813492739ef00.svg)" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186130403 :tutorial/content "Note the following important theorem.\n\n*The set of eigenvectors $\\{ \\mathbf{v}_1,\\mathbf{v}_2, \\ldots, \\mathbf{v}_k \\}$ of a matrix $A,$ where each vector $\\mathbf v_i$ corresponds to a different eigenvalue, is linearly independent.*\n\n**Watch out!** Eigenvectors that correspond to the *same* eigenvalue *can* be (and very often *will* be) linearly dependent." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186129325 :activity/expected-seconds 1020.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
